<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vidkun Quisling</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1887">1887</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vidkun Quisling was a Norwegian army officer and politician who founded the fascist party <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasjonal-samling">Nasjonal Samling</a> in 1933. On 9 April 1940, the day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, he proclaimed himself head of government; the Germans soon set him aside, but in 1942 made him minister-president under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichskommissar">Reichskommissar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josef-terboven">Josef Terboven</a>. He was tried after the war and executed for treason in 1945, and his name became a byword for traitor.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Quisling was born in Fyresdal, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telemark">Telemark</a>, the son of the pastor and genealogist Jon Lauritz Qvisling and Anna Caroline Bang, daughter of a Grimstad ship-owner. He first came to international prominence as a close collaborator of the explorer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fridtjof-nansen">Fridtjof Nansen</a>. In January 1922 he arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a> to help the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> relief effort during the Russian famine, and his report on the mismanagement and a death toll of about ten thousand a day attracted aid. He later served as a Norwegian diplomat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, where he also managed British diplomatic affairs for a time. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in December 1929 with a plan called Norsk Aktion, which proposed radical constitutional change.</p><p>Quisling was defence minister in the Agrarian governments of Peder Kolstad and Jens Hundseid from 1931 to 1933, though he was neither an Agrarian nor a friend of Kolstad. In 1933 he founded the fascist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasjonal-samling">Nasjonal Samling</a>, or National Gathering. He gained some popularity through attacks on the political left, but the party won no seats in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/storting">Storting</a>. In the summer of 1935 he told opponents that heads would roll once he achieved power, which damaged the party's image, and several high-ranking members, including his brother Jørgen, resigned. In 1939 he supported Hitler and toured German and Danish cities, where he was well received.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>During the German invasion on 9 April 1940 Quisling attempted the first radio-broadcast coup d'état, but the Germans sought instead to persuade the recognised government to legitimise the occupation. Hitler appointed Josef Terboven Reichskommissar on 24 April, and Terboven forced Quisling to step down as party leader in June. After Quisling won Hitler over on 16 August, he formed a second government on 1 February 1942 as minister president, alongside Terboven's administration. On 12 March 1942 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> officially became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>, and criticism of the party was criminalised. His collaborationist government took part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s war effort and deported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a> in occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Quisling turned himself in on 9 May 1945, and his trial opened on 20 August. He was found guilty of charges including embezzlement, murder and high treason, and was executed by firing squad at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akershus-fortress">Akershus Fortress</a> on 24 October 1945. Some criticised the sentence's legality, since no death sentence had been carried out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> since 1876. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasjonal-samling">Nasjonal Samling</a> was wiped out as a political force after 1945. The word quisling, coined by The Times in a leading article of 15 April 1940, became a synonym for traitor in several North European languages.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vidkun Quisling” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Vidkun Quisling</b>, leader of the fascist Nasjonal Samling, proclaimed himself head of government the same day, but the Germans soon pushed him aside.</blockquote><a href="/state/reichskommissariat-norwegen">Reichskommissariat Norwegen</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fyresdal Municipality, in what is now <a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akershus-fortress">Akershus Fortress</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.17,8.02&amp;year=1924" aria-label="Fyresdal Municipality on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Vidkun Quisling"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5350 5050h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5350 5050h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.17,8.02&amp;year=1924">What was here in 1924? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
